\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Nested blocks diagonalize the hierarchical precision}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{On the path with vertices #{0,1,2,3,4,5}, let #{\mu>0} and let the
precision be the path Laplacian plus #{\mu} on the diagonal. The
diagonal entries are #{\mu+1} at the two ends and #{\mu+2} at the
4 interior vertices, and each of the 5 edges contributes
#{-1}. Write #{m_0,m_1,m_2} for the averages of the three adjacent
pairs and #{d_0,d_1,d_2} for half the differences, so that the field
on pair #{i} is #{m_i+d_i} and #{m_i-d_i}. The change from
these coordinates, means before differences, to the path
coordinates has constant Jacobian #{8}. In these coordinates the quadratic form
has blocks
##{\begin{aligned}
H_{mm}&=\begin{pmatrix}2\mu+1&-1&0\\-1&2\mu+2&-1\\0&-1&2\mu+1\end{pmatrix},\\
B&=\begin{pmatrix}-1&-1&0\\1&0&-1\\0&1&1\end{pmatrix},\\
D&=\begin{pmatrix}2\mu+5&1&0\\1&2\mu+6&1\\0&1&2\mu+5\end{pmatrix},
\end{aligned}}
where #{B} holds the cross terms between means and differences. The
marginal precision of the three means is the Schur complement
#{H_{mm}-BD^{-1}B^{T}}.}

\p{The corner of #{H_{mm}} vanishes. The corner of
#{BD^{-1}B^{T}} is the pairing of #{(-1,-1,0)} with the solution of
#{D(x,y,z)^{T}=(0,1,1)^{T}}. The first row gives
#{y=-(2\mu+5)x}. The last row then gives
#{z=x+1/(2\mu+5)}. Substitution in the middle row produces
##{-2(\mu+2)(2\mu+7)x=\frac{2(\mu+2)}{2\mu+5},}
hence #{x=-1/((2\mu+5)(2\mu+7))} and
#{y=1/(2\mu+7)}. The pairing equals the corner of #{BD^{-1}B^{T}}:
##{-x-y=-\frac{2(\mu+2)}{(2\mu+5)(2\mu+7)}.}
Subtracting it from the zero corner of #{H_{mm}} gives the positive
quantity
##{\frac{2(\mu+2)}{(2\mu+5)(2\mu+7)}.}
A nearest-neighbour precision on the three coarse vertices would have
a zero corner. These pair means do not.}

\p{Let #{p\ge 2} and #{k\ge 1} be integers, and let the sites be the
residues modulo #{p^k}. For #{0\le j\le k} let #{E_j} replace the
value at each site by the mean of the sites congruent to it modulo
#{p^{k-j}}. Each such class has #{p^j} sites. Thus #{E_0} is the
identity, since congruence modulo #{p^k} isolates every residue, and
#{E_k} replaces every value by the mean of all the sites. Each
#{E_j} is symmetric, because its matrix is constant on the pairs
inside each class and zero across classes, and #{E_j^2=E_j}, because
a class mean is unchanged by a second average. If #{i\ge j},
congruence modulo #{p^{k-i}} is coarser than congruence modulo
#{p^{k-j}}, so each coarser class is a union of finer classes. A
function constant on a coarser class is constant on each finer class
inside it, and therefore #{E_jE_i=E_i}. That identity does not use
the sizes of the classes. For the other order, every finer class has
#{p^j} sites, so the mean on a coarser class is the mean of the finer
means and #{E_iE_j=E_i}.}

\p{In particular #{E_{j+1}E_j=E_jE_{j+1}=E_{j+1}}. Set
#{P_j=E_j-E_{j+1}} for #{j<k}, and #{P_k=E_k}. Then
#{P_jE_{j+1}=E_jE_{j+1}-E_{j+1}^2=E_{j+1}-E_{j+1}=0}. If #{i>j},
then #{i\ge j+1}. The size-free identity gives #{E_{j+1}E_i=E_i}, so
#{E_if=E_{j+1}(E_if)} and the image of #{E_i} lies in the image of
#{E_{j+1}}. When #{i<k} the same left factor gives
#{E_{j+1}E_{i+1}=E_{i+1}}, and both summands of #{P_i} land in that
image. When #{i=k}, #{E_{j+1}E_k=E_k}. A vector in the image of
#{P_i} is therefore #{E_{j+1}} applied to something, and
#{P_jE_{j+1}=0} gives #{P_jP_i=0}.
Both matrices are symmetric, so the opposite product vanishes as
well. The case #{P_k=E_k} is symmetric and idempotent because
#{E_k} is. For #{j<k}, the square expands to
#{E_j-E_jE_{j+1}-E_{j+1}E_j+E_{j+1}^2}, and the relations replace
that by #{E_j-E_{j+1}-E_{j+1}+E_{j+1}=P_j}. Symmetry of #{P_j} is
the same property of the two averages. The sum of the projectors
telescopes to #{E_0}, since #{E_0} is the identity. These matrices
are orthogonal projectors, they are pairwise orthogonal, and they
sum to the identity. For real coefficients #{c_0,\ldots,c_k}, the
hierarchical precision
##{Q=\sum_{j=0}^{k}c_jP_j}
acts on the image of #{P_j} by multiplication by #{c_j}. Whenever
every coefficient is nonzero, #{Q} is invertible and its inverse
multiplies that same image by #{c_j^{-1}}.}
